.” […] The following quote suggests that there were religious iconographic exchanges between Protopalatial Crete and Egypt and that Minoans adapted these images for their own religious purposes. “Another clear inst2ance of iconographic transfer from the supernatural realm is the figure of Taweret, a hippopotamus goddess associated with women and childbirth in Egypt. She appears on Crete in Protopalatial times, and continued in later periods, but we cannot be sure if the Minoans kept her original religious meaning when they borrowed and changed her image.” […] NB the following quote postulates that the name of a potential Minoan goddess might be related to a Hittite or Levantine goddess with a similar name but is not known for sure. “[…] the early Knossos sealings were impressed by seals bearing the name ‘JA-SA-SA-RA’ in the script commonly known as Cretan Hieroglyphic; JA-SA-SA-RA may be the name of a goddess akin to the Hittite ‘Esha-sara’ or the Levantine ‘Asherah’.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1803, 'temperature': 0.2})
